\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We formalize evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction as a structured generation task and systematically compare three post-training paradigms across three fully evaluated scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B) with 14B validation, two model families, and two datasets. Our experiments reveal that evidence generation plays a scale-dependent role shaped by three interacting factors: model capacity appears to determine whether evidence acts as regularizer ($-$5.63pp at 1.7B) or is approximately neutral ($\geq$4B; +0.25$\pm$0.47pp at 4B) within the Qwen3 family, optimization paradigm determines decoupling severity (GRPO catastrophic under QLoRA, DPO gradual, RSFT none), and data quality filtering through NLI-based scoring enables RSFT to navigate this landscape without gradient-induced trade-offs. RSFT improves mean Ign-F1 across tested scales (+1.2 to +6.6pp) while compressing Qwen3 training variance 7.5--17$\times$ and LLaMA variance 4.6$\times$ (complete pairwise seed separation, $d{=}2.58$). The resulting 1.7B RSFT model (51.19 Ign-F1, std 0.09pp) approaches 8B SFT performance (50.19, std 3.23pp) at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters, providing both higher mean accuracy and substantially greater training reliability for deployment-efficient evidence-grounded extraction.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We formalize evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction as a structured generation task and systematically compare three post-training paradigms across three fully evaluated scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B) with 14B validation, two model families, and two datasets. Our experiments reveal that evidence generation plays a scale-dependent role shaped by three interacting factors: model capacity appears to determine whether evidence acts as regularizer ($-$5.63pp at 1.7B) or is approximately neutral ($\geq$4B; +0.25$\pm$0.47pp at 4B) within the Qwen3 family, optimization paradigm determines decoupling severity (GRPO catastrophic under QLoRA, DPO gradual, RSFT none), and data quality filtering through NLI-based scoring enables RSFT to navigate this landscape without gradient-induced trade-offs. RSFT improves mean Ign-F1 across tested scales (+1.2 to +6.6pp) while compressing Qwen3 training variance 7.5--17$\times$ and LLaMA variance 4.6$\times$ (complete pairwise seed separation, $d{=}2.58$). The resulting 1.7B RSFT model (51.19 Ign-F1, std 0.09pp) approaches 8B SFT performance (50.19, std 3.23pp) at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters, providing both higher mean accuracy and substantially greater training reliability for deployment-efficient evidence-grounded extraction.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Dataset.}
We conduct experiments on DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred}, a large-scale document-level relation extraction benchmark containing 3,053 training and 998 development documents spanning 96 relation types. Each document requires identifying relational triples across multiple sentences and grounding each triple with supporting evidence sentences. We use Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred} for cross-dataset generalization evaluation.

\paragraph{Models.}
Our primary model is Qwen3-4B. To study scale effects, we additionally train Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B under identical configurations. We include LLaMA-3.1-8B as a cross-family validation point.

\paragraph{Training.}
All models use LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} with rank $r{=}64$ and $\alpha{=}128$ over a structured output format that jointly produces relation triples and evidence sentences. For RSFT, we sample $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per document and select the top-$k{=}1$ response ranked by CED scoring (\S\ref{sec:ced}), which combines NLI-based evidence faithfulness with extraction accuracy. GRPO experiments use group sizes $G{=}4$ (QLoRA, 4-bit) and $G{=}8$ (bf16 full-precision LoRA). DPO constructs preference pairs from CED-ranked outputs.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
\textbf{Ign-F1} (primary) ignores training-set triples per the official DocRED protocol. \textbf{Rel-F1} measures extraction F1 on all triples. \textbf{Evi-F1 (TP-only)} measures evidence F1 conditioned on correct triples; \textbf{Evi-F1 (joint)} follows the DREEAM protocol~\citep{ma2023dreeam} for cross-method comparison. \textbf{EDCR} (\S\ref{sec:edcr}) diagnoses extraction-evidence decoupling. All results use the development set.


\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison on Qwen3-4B. The SFT baseline exhibits high seed sensitivity (mean Ign-F1 43.58, std 3.80pp, range 39.74--47.34). Removing evidence entirely (no-evidence SFT, s42) reaches 47.21 Ign-F1.

RSFT CED achieves 44.82 Ign-F1 (+1.24 over SFT mean) with substantially lower variance (std 0.51pp), though these reflect different sources of variation (Section~\ref{sec:scaling}). Even the weakest RSFT seed (44.23) exceeds the SFT mean. Evidence quality improves (Evi-F1 80.96 vs.\ 79.61) and coupling tightens (EDCR 0.6487 vs.\ 0.6797). Per-seed results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}.

